Certainly! Below is an example of how you might configure BGP and OSPF on Juniper devices, along with applying an access list to control traffic.

### BGP Configuration

```junos
set protocols bgp group my-bgp {
    type external;
    local-as none;
}

set protocols bgp group my-bgp neighbor 192.168.1.1 remote-as 65000;
```

### OSPF Configuration

```junos
set protocols ospf area 0.0.0.0 router-id 192.168.1.1;

set protocols ospf area 0.0.0.0 interface ge-0/0/0.0;
set protocols ospf area 0.0.0.0 interface ge-0/0/1.0;

set protocols ospf area 64 router-id 192.168.1.1;

set protocols ospf area 64 interface ge-0/0/0.0;
set protocols ospf area 64 interface ge-0/0/1.0;
```

### Access List Configuration

Assuming you want to apply an access list named `control-traffic` to control traffic between the two networks, here’s how you can do it:

```junos
set security policies from-zone trust to-zone untrust policy control-traffic match source-address 10.13.0.0/24
set security policies from-zone trust to-zone untrust policy control-traffic match destination-address 10.14.0.0/24
set security policies from-zone trust to-zone untrust policy control-traffic then permit
```

### Full Configuration Example

Here is the complete configuration for both BGP and OSPF, including the access list:

```junos
set system name "example-juniper"

set interfaces ge-0/0/0 unit 0 family inet address 192.168.1.1/24
set interfaces ge-0/0/1 unit 0 family inet address 192.168.1.2/24

set protocols bgp group my-bgp {
    type external;
    local-as none;
}

set protocols bgp group my-bgp neighbor 192.168.1.1 remote-as 65000;

set protocols ospf area

